= Content model =

A canonical knowledge page is both human-readable and machine-queryable.

== Page model ==
A page may be understood as:

; Identity
: namespace, entity type, domain, optional scope, and semantic relationships.

; Content
: coherent prose organized into reusable Book Matter.

; Epistemics
: citations, source provenance, epistemic status, confidence, uncertainty, and verification information where useful.

; Presentation
: templates, modules, portals, graphs, and generated navigation.

== Knowledge lifecycle ==
Useful work may begin in discussion, research, collaboration, or computational workflows. After review and refinement, durable results can be crystallized into structured BITwiki knowledge. The objective is not to archive every interaction but to preserve reusable signal.

== Content quality ==
V2 favors content that is:
* informative without unnecessary repetition;
* explicit about evidence and uncertainty;
* modular without becoming fragmented;
* sophisticated while remaining legible;
* reusable across human and machine contexts;
* clear about perspective and provenance;
* connected to related knowledge where relationships are meaningful.

== Pluralism ==
BITwiki does not need to pretend that every topic has one perspective. Multiple lenses can coexist when they are distinguishable, sourced, and represented without turning metaphor or interpretation into unsupported fact.

